				<div class="post-676 post hentry category-uncategorized">
			<h2 id="post-676"><a href="http://blog.cromwellhaus.com/index.php/2007/04/personal-backup-with-mozy/" rel="bookmark" title="Permanent Link to Personal Backup with Mozy">Personal Backup with Mozy</a></h2>
			<small class="meta">April 27th, 2007 </small>

			<div class="entry">
				<p>Relatively speaking, I&#8217;ve been one lucky computer user.&nbsp; I&#8217;ve never had a hard drive fail here at the house.&nbsp; I&#8217;ve never accidentally deleted something vital.&nbsp; I even&nbsp;believed in the&nbsp;annual-reimage.&nbsp; As far as losing data I&#8217;ve never really lost anything I cared that much about.&nbsp; For that reason, I&#8217;m only now looking at a serious backup &#8220;strategy&#8221;.&nbsp; I put &#8220;strategy&#8221; in quotes, because DVD&#8217;s and CD&#8217;s do not suffice as a strategy.&nbsp; It&#8217;s a good step that should be used in a strategy, but by itself you do not have a strategy.</p>
<p>Now that more than just I rely on our financial data being in order (I&#8217;m a huge Quicken believer) and we have more pictures of our <a href="http://cromwellhaus.com/photos/baby/">3 month old sun</a>&nbsp;than the previous 28 years of our lives combined I have a vested interest in not losing it all.</p>
<p>I&#8217;ve been mulling my options now for about 3 or 4 months; playing with different tools like <a href="http://www.jungledisk.com/">Jungle Disk</a>, <a href="http://www.microsoft.com/windows/products/windowsvista/features/details/backup.mspx">Vista Backup and Restore</a>, <a href="http://www.microsoft.com/windows/products/winfamily/windowshomeserver/default.mspx">Windows Home Server,</a> <a href="http://mozy.com/">Mozy</a>, and several others.&nbsp; </p>
			</div>

